  q(HS,'A patient has a procedure and goes home the same day without an overnight stay. What type of care is this?','Outpatient (ambulatory) care',['Inpatient (acute) care','Hospice (end-of-life) care','Long-term residential care'],'B','Ambulatory surgery centers provide many outpatient procedures.',DHO),
  q(HS,'Which part of Medicare helps pay for outpatient prescription drugs?','Part D',['Part A','Part B','Part C'],'C','Part A covers hospital stays, Part B covers doctor visits and Part C is Medicare Advantage.',DHO),
  q(HS,'Which federal department oversees agencies such as the CDC, FDA and NIH?','Health and Human Services (HHS)',['Department of Defense (DOD)','Department of Education (ED)','Department of Labor (DOL)'],'D','HHS is the main federal department for protecting health and providing services.',DHO),
  q(HC,'Which professional evaluates and treats swallowing problems and speech disorders?','Speech-language pathologist',['Audiologist','Respiratory therapist','Registered dietitian nutritionist'],'A','Speech-language pathologists work in schools, hospitals and rehabilitation centers.',DHO),
  q(HC,'Which professional prepares the operating room and passes instruments to the surgeon?','Surgical technologist',['Phlebotomist','Medical assistant','Sterile processing clerk'],'B','Surgical technologists help keep the sterile field safe during procedures.',DHO),
  q(HC,'Which licensed professional examines eyes and prescribes glasses and contact lenses?','Optometrist',['Optician','Ophthalmic assistant','Audiologist'],'C','Opticians fill eyeglass prescriptions, while ophthalmologists are medical doctors.',DHO),
  q(LE,'Negligence, assault and invasion of privacy are all examples of what kind of civil wrong?','Torts',['Crimes only','Contracts','Felonies'],'D','A tort is a wrong against a person or property that can lead to a civil lawsuit.',DHO),
  q(LE,'What sets the time limit for filing a lawsuit after an injury?','The statute of limitations',['The scope of practice','HIPAA','Good Samaritan laws'],'A','Time limits vary by state and by type of case, and after they pass a lawsuit is usually barred.',DHO),
  q(LE,'In most states, who must give consent for routine care of a 12-year-old patient?','A parent or legal guardian',['The child alone','Any adult in the room','The school nurse'],'B','Exceptions exist in some states for emancipated minors and certain types of care.',DHO),
  q(NUT,'The Dietary Guidelines for Americans advise adults to limit sodium to less than how much per day?','2,300 mg',['500 mg','1,000 mg','5,000 mg'],'C','Too much sodium can raise blood pressure, and most sodium comes from processed foods.',DHO),
  q(NUT,'A protein that contains all nine essential amino acids is called:','A complete protein',['An incomplete protein','A simple sugar','A trans fat'],'D','Animal foods and soy are complete proteins, while most plant foods must be combined.',DHO),
  q(SAF,'A patient is receiving oxygen. Which safety rule is most important?','No smoking or open flames nearby',['Keep the room dark and quiet','Turn off the call light','Remove the bed rails to give space'],'A','Oxygen supports combustion, so even a small spark can start a fire.',DHO),
  q(SAF,'What should a health worker do right after using a needle?','Put it in a sharps container, uncapped',['Recap it carefully using both hands','Put it in the regular trash bag','Bend or break it before disposal'],'B','Recapping is a common cause of needlestick injuries, so sharps go straight into the container.',DHO),
  q(INF,'When removing PPE, which item does the CDC sequence remove first?','Gloves',['Gown','Mask','Goggles'],'C','Gloves are most contaminated, so they are removed first and hands are cleaned at the end.',DHO),
  q(INF,'A patient with active tuberculosis needs airborne precautions. Which type of room is used?','A negative-pressure isolation room',['A room with the door open','A shared room with a curtain','A positive-pressure room'],'D','Negative pressure keeps air from flowing out of the room into the hallway.',DHO),
  q(MATH,'How many micrograms are in 0.5 milligram?','500 mcg',['5 mcg','50 mcg','5,000 mcg'],'A','There are 1,000 micrograms in 1 milligram, so 0.5 × 1,000 = 500 mcg.',DHO),
  q(MATH,'A patient is 5 feet 6 inches tall. About how tall is this in centimeters?','168 cm',['132 cm','155 cm','198 cm'],'B','5 feet 6 inches = 66 inches, and one inch is 2.54 cm, so 66 × 2.54 ≈ 168 cm.',DHO)
